g time  we're talking about china's nine dash  line back in 1929 the chinese took a  bunch of  old british sailing charts and told  cartographers to draw china's borders  one of them drew nine dashes around the  entire south china sea  and as far as the chinese were concerned  that was that  over the last 70 plus years the line's  taken on a life of its own and  in beijing at least the meaning has  steadily expanded so that today  china's not just claiming the islands  inside it it's claiming all the waters  all the seabed all the airspace  so then in 2013 china added a tenth line  to take in taiwan  its appetite seems to be growing it's  easy enough to understand  why china wants control when you look at  the numbers around  40 percent of the world's liquefied  natural gas passes  through the south china sea from the  gulf through the indian  ocean and onto places like china the  amount of trade that sails through there  is worth three trillion dollars a year  one third of the world total  we am able to control this waterway what  we normally call the sea lions of  communication is extremely important for  china  so there's an expression in latin that  goes if you want peace  prepare for war now china has no  immediate interest in war  but it wants to ensure its access to the  south china sea  if it comes to that in the west the main  shipping lane from the indian ocean  narrows to just  three kilometers and it's surrounded by  american allies  in the east there's taiwan which has  tons of american weapons  and could block china from getting to  the pacific so to help stake its  territorial claim  china has planted its flag on contested  islands that are  in those waterways  in the paracels china's built a bunch of  outposts  while in the spratlys it's added over 13  square kilometers of reclaimed land  since 2013  and turned them into military bases the  militarization of the south china sea  has grown  rapidly over the last five six seven  years it has come to a point where  it's unacceptable for united states it  may be unacceptable to the americans  but neither the obama nor the trump  administrations are seen to have  accomplished much  the us's main way of asserting  navigational rights and freedoms  is having its navy sail through the  south china sea  it also conducts military exercises in  the region sometimes with warships from  france japan and australia in the bay of  bengal  in july things got a bit hairy the  chinese and americans both  held naval exercises in the south china  sea worsening tensions between china  and the united states the u.s sailed in  with two aircraft carrier strike groups  the first time washington sent two  carriers for drills  in the area since 2014. and the chinese  accused the americans of escalating  things before the u.s elections the  trump team gave it  one last shot with mixed results  [Music]  don't let the chinese communist party  walk over us  you should have confidence that america  will  what everyone's been trying to do is get  china to obey the law  namely the un convention on the law of  the sea  the law establishes what are called  exclusive economic zones  these extend 200 nautical miles out from  the country's coastline  regardless of whether it's the mainland  or an island and china signed up to that  law  all of the world came together including  china  in the 1970s and negotiated the current  regime of maritime law  which says everybody gets the same set  of maritime claims  china's claims don't fit within that  framework  so the philippines did the only thing it  could it took china to court  one of the big issues was fishing rights  for millions of filipinos living in  coastal  communities like this one food security  is at the heart  of the south china sea dispute in 2013  the philippines argued that china had no  right to the scarborough shoal  it filed a case against the chinese with  an international tribunal at the hague  judges ended up siding with the  philippines remember china's territorial  claim based on that nine-dash line  well in 2016 the court concluded that  it's invalid  when after china lost a landmark  arbitration case before the philippines  beijing realized that the best way to  divert attention from that and avoid  diplomatic blowback  would be to show some progress since the  1990s  asean that association of south asian  countries has tried to cobble together a  code of conduct for the south china sea  the idea was to control china's  ambitions  china hadn't shown much interest in a  code back then  but after losing the case with the  philippines the chinese started to push  for one  though some in asean questioned china's  motives  honestly and unfortunately i don't think  there is any  fertile ground for reaching an agreement  it's not trying to solve anything  right the code of conduct will not say  who owns what rock  it won't say whose maritime claims go  where  it's meant to manage disputes there's  another problem  not everyone in asean is eager to offend  a country as powerful as china  certain members of the grouping  particularly cambodia but also  laos at times thailand have really no  desire  to irritate china and so this means a  whole heck of a lot to  four of the members five maybe and  almost nothing to the other five  and that's a recipe for no progress  vietnam has repeatedly butted heads with  china over their competing claims  the last time there was a real talk was  in dalat vietnam  and the vietnamese delegation blew up  the whole process because they were so  angry  over recent harassment of oil and gas  operations by china that had gone on at  the same time  so they'll say that there's progress i  would encourage everybody to take that  with a giant grain of salt this year's  asean summit was hosted by vietnam  and a lot of it by video because of the  pandemic but the social distancing  wasn't expected to help delegates reach  any agreements on the south china sea  we expressed serious concern over  ongoing developments that infringe on  the lawful rights  of literal countries the problem with  the south china sea is that it's vast  and the stakes are high  there are a lot of heavily armed  countries defending their interests  there  and china's coast guard as well as  chinese militia using fishing boats  often cause confrontations on the open  sea  many analysts worry about all of that  provoking a military response  from someone but strangely enough the  whole dispute also sort of just bubbles  away  under the radar the south china sea is  so insidious  it is hard to keep attention on it not  just within washington but  all of the other global partners and  allies somebody is always going to be  distracted  or find it not all that important the  chinese see the south china sea as  essential to their survival thing is  it's important to everyone there  that's why this whole dispute isn't  going away but china's been around  for thousands of years if anyone's good  at playing the long game  it's china  as with all our star here episodes this  primer is to get us started  but there's a lot more information out  there to help us understand what's going  on in the south china sea  as always subscribe wherever you're  watching this video and i'll see you  next week
 

